Architectural scaling notes

1. You don’t want to average percentiles across different endpoints.  For example, 10 requests to target 1, 10 request to target 2, if you calculate the p95 of them individually and average them, this can cause huge problems, for instance if one has a p95 of 10ms, and one has a p95 of 100ms, averaged they’re 50ms, which is inaccurate.  Instead, take the buckets across both nodes, and calculate the p95 from that total, to find the accurate p95 for all nodes.     Service Discovery is used instead of static targets with multiple systems,, and promql operations need to be instance aware.  For example, running a query on a single instance will now introduce multiple responses because prometheus adds instance labels which can mess with alerts if you want to alert on totals. so in this instance you would sum across instances

2. Cardinality Math unbounded labels can not only be added to code, but added in with telemetry libraries or in other places not directly in code.  Prometheus has scrape/series limits which aren’t that good long term since they just show a target as down instead of resolving the problem.  (They drop information and the dashboard goes down until it goes back under the limits)
    
3. If you have multiple prometheus (multi cluster, or need multiple prometheus servers for some reason) it doesn’t aggregate properly, for small setups you can have a parent preomethus federate them, but this doesn’t scale well.


